\subsection{Geometric Analysis}
\label{sec:geometric}

We quantify cross-lingual representational similarity with Centered Kernel Alignment (CKA) \citep{kornblith2019cka}. % [TO_ADD_BIB]
The central analysis applies partial correlation to control for layer depth, which reveals a Simpson's Paradox in the CKA-transfer relationship. The raw correlation between CKA and transfer AUROC across all language-layer pairs is negative, because both quantities covary with depth (deeper layers have lower CKA and, in some architectures, lower transfer AUROC). After controlling for depth, the partial correlation reverses sign: within each layer, higher cross-lingual CKA predicts better probe transfer (Llama pilot: $\rho = +0.41$, $p = 0.006$; 14 of 17 layer-level correlations positive). We report bootstrap confidence intervals (1000 resamples) for all partial correlations. We supplement CKA with subspace angle analysis that measures the cross-lingual angular distance between correctness directions $\mathbf{w}_l$ at each layer. These results are framed as geometric and correlational analysis, not mechanistic explanation.

\subsection{Probing Controls}
\label{sec:controls}

We validate probe quality with the control task framework of \citet{hewitt2019control}. % [TO_ADD_BIB]
Selectivity is the gap between AUROC on true correctness labels and AUROC on randomly shuffled labels; higher selectivity indicates that the probe captures genuine task-relevant structure rather than exploiting representational capacity. We reduce activations to 100 dimensions via PCA before probe training to guard against high-dimensional overfitting, and report 5-fold cross-validated estimates. Pilot results on Qwen2.5-7B show selectivity concentrated in layers 20 through 27 (0.244 to 0.262), with shallow layers below 0.05. Across the full language-layer grid, approximately 28\% of low-resource combinations fall below a meaningful selectivity threshold, which indicates that a substantial fraction of reported probing results in these settings reflect capacity artifacts rather than genuine correctness encoding.
